00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:01.829 align:start position:0%
 
now as we've been discussing this  morning the reason the chancellor's in  such trouble over his family's tax and  residence arrangement  is how all this looks at a time when so  many people's finances are being  squeezed sometimes by decisions he  himself is taking  ad work benefits will rise by a little  over three percent tomorrow whilst  inflation is heading north of eight  percent that's a very significant cut in  income and living standards for some of  the uh those on the lowest incomes in  the country in fact the joseph rowntree  foundation says it will be the greatest  fall in the value of basic rate of  out-of-work benefits in 50 years  joseph roundry foundation director helen  barnard joins me now  good morning mrs barnard um  why do you say this is the biggest fall  uh in such a long period  what we've seen obviously is for a  decade we've had cuts and freezes to  benefits and actually for eight of the  last 10 years the benefits have been  losing value  so we're already at a historic low  now because although benefits go up  tomorrow they go up by so much less than  the cost of living is rising  it means that in terms of their value so  how much bread and milk you can buy in  the shops it is the biggest fall in  value since 1972 for 50 years  and obviously people are already very  often in quite desperate situations they  are struggling to afford food and  heating and basic hygiene products  and now the government is imposing this  enormous cut  probably at the worst possible time  given how difficult things are out there  in the country  uh from your research what what um  can you describe the real world impact  of this to some of the families that  you've talked to  i mean we're seeing things like  pensioners riding the buses all day to  keep warm we're seeing children who are  using a torch at night because even at  age six they know their parents can't  afford to turn the lights on all the  time  we've heard from food banks who are  saying look please don't donate fresh  food because the people we're helping  can't afford to plug the fridge in let  alone cook food  there was a story the other day about a  young man a teenager fainting at a food  bank because he hadn't eaten enough in  the run-up  and actually we're seeing this spread so  people who have been donating and  volunteering for years  are actually having to say at the end of  their shift have we got any left over  because i can't afford to eat properly  anymore  i don't know if you had the chance to  listen to um the crime policing minister  kit malthouse when i spoke to him about  what 45 minutes ago and asked him about  this and he said that  in essence the government had no choice  after  covered and the amount was spent there  the uncertainties for the public  finances uh engendered by the conflict  uh in ukraine um  what's your answer to  that uh case  i think if you look at the spring  statement the chancellor spent about 18  billion pounds that's the money that he  decided he could afford to spend he  probably could have spent more and still  stayed within his rules but let's just  take the 18 billion  of that money about two-thirds went to  benefit people in the richest half of  the country  so he actually had the money the issue  is that the government is not  prioritizing using the money they have  to protect the poorest people who are  facing the greatest hardship  your report today is about people who  are dependent on benefits but um the  squeeze isn't just facing those people i  think um people in work are also facing  uh some uh drop in their living  standards um  what what does uh your organization  uh think is going to happen to those in  work and what the what will be the  impact on them  of course many people at work also get  benefits to top up low earnings so this  squeeze on benefits actually affects a  lot of low-income workers as well as  people who can't work because they are  disabled or they're caring for small  children or they're caring for adults so  actually workers are going to feel this  and of course it's this kind of irony  that even where you have something like  raising the national insurance threshold  which helps some not all but some  low-income workers  people who are earning little enough  that they get topped up by benefit  system actually lose a lot of the  benefit of something like that  because their benefits get pulled away  to make up for the fact that they're  keeping a bit more of their income  so actually low-income workers  are facing incredibly difficult  situations  and you know we know the majority of  people in poverty now are in working  households one of the problems is that  too many jobs are not just low paid but  they're insecure you know you don't know  what money you're getting one week to  the next you don't get sick pay  you don't get protection if something  goes wrong  and so it is people who are really  vulnerable in that situation when  they're working who are also now  struggling to afford you know the basic  essentials and having to rely on  charities or you know toothpaste and  toilet roll and you know it it's  humiliating for a lot of people you know  wonderful as it is that people are  helping  to know that even if you're working two  jobs you still can't afford to turn your  heating on or by your child a pair of  shoes that fit them that's not a  situation anyone should be in  helen barnard thank you very much for  your time this morning

